"The postoperative pathology reveals a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) and pathological stage pT2c. Adverse findings include positive surgical margins and lymphovascular invasion, as noted in the surgical pathology report: \"surgical margins were positive; the seminal vesicles were not invaded; lymphovascular invasion was present; no lymph nodes were removed.\" The seminal vesicles were not invaded, which is a reassuring finding. The status of the lymph nodes is uncertain as no nodes were removed, meaning \"pNx is not pN0.\" Preoperative PSA is not recorded, and postoperative PSA is also unknown, which prevents assessment of biochemical recurrence. The documented positive margins and lymphovascular invasion represent adverse prognostic factors. Countervailing findings include the absence of extraprostatic extension and non-invasion of the seminal vesicles. Information regarding postoperative PSA and its trajectory is crucial for revising the prognosis. Missing nodal sampling widens uncertainty regarding the true extent of disease. The fixed estimated horizon is 59.37 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 35.62–83.12 months; its coverage has not been validated."
